{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 51, :comments ["<b>And when the tabernacle journeys, the Levites shall take it down, and when the tabernacle encamps, the Levites shall erect it, and the stranger who draws near shall die.</b> I have already informed you that wherever you find the Name inscribed in reverse, with its letters inverted, it indicates the attribute of judgment — for thus Haman the wicked mentioned it with its letters inverted, saying (Esther 5:13): ״וכל זה איננו שוה לי״ — and the attribute of judgment was turned upon him. Now here, in the matter of the service of the Levites, Scripture first mentions the inconceivable Name, and afterward mentions the explicit Name twice in reverse, to indicate the attribute of judgment that is stretched over the stranger who draws near to the service of the Levites — as happened to Uzzah, of whom it is written (II Samuel 6:7): ״ויחר אף ה׳ בעזה״." "<b>And observe</b> how the hint begins each time from the word הלוים — from what you know, that the attribute of Levi is the attribute of judgment, and it is the final ה of the Name; therefore the hint of the ה begins each time from the word הלוים, and this is clear. <br>The meaning of the verse <b>shall take it down</b> — they remove the bars and the pillars, and when it encamped they would erect it. <b>And the stranger who draws near</b>, to the service, <b>shall die</b>, by the hand of Heaven."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The verse text embeds reversed instances of the Tetragrammaton (formed by marking letters in ה'לוים, ה'משכן, etc.); the translation preserves the lemma as a unit while comment [0] explains the reversal. Citation normalized: שמואל ב = II Samuel."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:41.138939337Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}